6. AI_INSTRUCTIONS.md

This is the primary operating guide for AI agents working on the repository.

Create a clear section called:

# AI Operating Instructions

Include these principles.

6.1 Read before modifying

An AI agent must inspect the relevant project-control files before making substantive changes.

At minimum, normally check:

README.md
AI_INSTRUCTIONS.md
PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.md
PROJECT_STATUS.md
WORKFLOW.md
DECISIONS.md

Then read the relevant stage-specific documents.

For research work, also consult the relevant research-control documents.

For manuscript work, consult the relevant editorial and chapter specifications.

Do not load every file unnecessarily.

---

6.2 Do not guess

If the repository contains an established rule, decision, requirement or verified research finding, follow it.

If information is missing:

- identify the gap;
- do not invent an answer;
- do not silently substitute an assumption;
- report the uncertainty.

---

6.3 Preserve source research

Raw research and source material must be preserved.

Do not replace raw research with a rewritten summary.

Derived documents may synthesise research, but the underlying source material should remain available.

---

6.4 Distinguish evidence types

Never silently turn:

- forecasts into actuals;
- targets into achievements;
- historical data into current data;
- estimates into verified facts;
- registered accounts into active users;
- qualitative claims into numerical statistics.

---

6.5 Scope discipline

Only perform the task requested.

Do not take the initiative to start later project stages merely because they appear possible.

If a task reveals another problem, record it and report it unless fixing it is necessary to complete the current task.

---

6.6 Review before committing

Before committing:

1. inspect changed files;
2. review the diff;
3. check for accidental changes;
4. verify filenames;
5. check Markdown formatting;
6. confirm no research files were unintentionally changed;
7. confirm no unrelated files were modified.

---

6.7 Report unresolved issues

Every meaningful task report should identify:

- what was done;
- files changed;
- files created;
- files not changed;
- assumptions;
- unresolved issues;
- validation performed;
- commit ID;
- next recommended action.

